0.1054 Tc 0.116 Tw (The bit-by-bit arbitration mechanism allows the implementation of a fram\e priority system.)Tj
0.1168 Tc 0.3602 Tw -66 -12 Td
(When unique frames or, more particularly, when arbitration is resolved d\own to a single transmitter prior to)Tj
0.0865 Tc 0.03 Tw T*
(any data values within the frame, a structure of relative frame priority\ is inherent.)Tj
0.1231 Tc 0.682 Tw 0 -24 TD
(Should two or more nodes attempt to access the bus within the same frame\ synchronization window \(see)Tj
0.1008 Tc 0.9387 Tw 0 -12 TD
(IFS in 8.6.1.5 and 8.6.2.7\), nondestructive arbitration will occur base\d on the bit value within each frame.)Tj
0.0953 Tc 0.2656 Tw T*
(The bus allows two states - active and passive.  If an active state bit \and a passive state bit are transmitted)Tj
0.0752 Tc 2.3761 Tw T*
(on the bus simultaneously, then the active state bit will override the p\assive state bit.  Hence, frame)Tj
0.0949 Tc 0.0696 Tw T*
(arbitration occurs in a bit-wise manner.  The result of the arbitration \is that the node\(s\) sending lower priority)Tj
0.0867 Tc 1.4028 Tw T*
(frame\(s\) will recognize that they have lost arbitration and will disco\ntinue transmitting before the next bit)Tj
0.0842 Tc 0.0803 Tw T*
(time.  The node transmitting the highest priority frame will continue tr\ansmitting uninterrupted.)Tj
