0.1126 Tc 3.3912 Tw (The \223In-Frame Response\224 \(IFR\) is transmitted by the)Tj
0.0995 Tc 0.19 Tw 0 -12 TD
(responder and begins after the passive EOD symbol.  For Variable Pulse W\idth Modulation, the first bit of)Tj
0.1105 Tc 0.2974 Tw T*
(the IFR data is also passive.  Therefore, it is necessary to generate a \normalization bit to follow the EOD)Tj
0.101 Tc 1.9697 Tw T*
(symbol.  The responding device generates the normalization bit prior to \sending the IFR data.  This)Tj
0.1165 Tc -0.3145 Tw T*
(normalization bit defines the start of the IFR and can take two forms. T\he first form is an active short period)Tj
0.1145 Tc 2.425 Tw T*
(\(Tv1\) and the second form is an active long period \(Tv2\).  Figure 18\ illustrates the IFR using the)Tj
0.0981 Tc 0.809 Tw T*
(normalization bit.  The normalization bit can also be used to indicate w\hat type of response is expected)Tj
0.0916 Tc -0.195 Tw T*
(during the IFR portion of the frame.  The preferred method is to use an \active short bit \(Tv1\) to indicate that)Tj
0.0931 Tc 1.5839 Tw T*
(the IFR DOES NOT contain a CRC \(i.e., IFR types 1 or 2\).  An active lo\ng bit \(Tv2\) would therefore)Tj
0.1233 Tc 1.8241 Tw T*
(indicate that the IFR DOES contain a CRC \(i.e., IFR type 3\).  This is \only a preferred method, and)Tj
0.1104 Tc 0.1214 Tw T*
(individual manufacturers are allowed to implement the normalization bit \per their requirements.  However,)Tj
0.1211 Tc -0.0816 Tw T*
(all future SAE J1850 applications are urged to implement the normalizati\on bit using the preferred method)Tj
0.1809 Tc -0.6414 Tw T*
(described above.)Tj
0.1191 Tc 0.8266 Tw 0 -24 TD
(If in-frame response bytes are used, the overall frame/message length li\mit remains in effect.  The sum)Tj
0.0906 Tc 0.1106 Tw 0 -12 TD
(total of data bytes, CRC byte, and in-frame response bytes shall not exc\eed the value specified in Section)Tj
